1. Firebase Authentication
You need a secure way to verify users and link their data.

Authentication Methods: Phone Number OTP (Crucial for a call-related app to verify the user's actual device number) and Google Sign-In.

Stored Data: Firebase Auth automatically handles unique User IDs (UID), which we will use to link our databases.

2. Cloud Firestore (The Main Database)
Firestore is perfect here because of its structured, document-based layout. It allows you to easily query fraud numbers and user profiles.

Here is the recommended collection structure:

users (Collection)
Stored when a user registers. Holds profile info and emergency contacts.

Document ID: UID (From Firebase Auth)

name: String

phone_number: String

emergency_contacts: Array (List of phone numbers to alert during danger)

created_at: Timestamp

fraud_registry (Collection)
The core of your detective system. This holds the database of reported fraud/scam numbers.

Document ID: phone_number (The reported phone number formatted with country code, e.g., +919876543210)

report_count: Integer (Increments every time a new user reports it)

risk_level: String ("Low", "Medium", "High" based on the report count)

tags: Array (e.g., ["Scam", "Harassment", "Fake Delivery"])

last_reported: Timestamp

user_reports (Collection)
Keeps track of who reported what, preventing a single user from spamming the "Report" button on the same number.

Document ID: Auto-generated ID

reported_by: String (UID)

target_number: String

reason: String

timestamp: Timestamp

3. Realtime Database (For Emergency Triggers)
While Firestore is great for the fraud registry, the Firebase Realtime Database (RTDB) is better for instant, low-latency triggers—like a "Panic Button" or an automated distress signal if a dangerous fraud call is intercepted.

live_emergencies (Node)
UID (User's ID)

is_in_danger: Boolean (true / false)

current_location: GeoPoint (latitude, longitude)

last_updated: Timestamp

How it works in Java: Your app can listen to the fraud_registry using Firestore, but if the user hits an SOS button, it writes to RTDB. The app can instantly sync the location coordinates to emergency contacts.

