To make this work seamlessly in the background without locking up the user's screen, we will use a BroadcastReceiver to capture the incoming phone number, and a background thread to safely query Firebase Firestore without lagging the device.

1. The Java Code (CallReceiver.java)
Create a new Java class named CallReceiver.java. This script listens for incoming phone states, grabs the number, and checks it against your Firestore fraud_registry collection.

Java
package com.safeher.app; // Change this to your actual package name

import android.content.BroadcastReceiver;
import android.content.Context;
import android.content.Intent;
import android.telephony.TelephonyManager;
import android.util.Log;
import android.widget.Toast;

import androidx.annotation.NonNull;

import com.google.android.gms.tasks.OnCompleteListener;
import com.google.android.gms.tasks.Task;
import com.google.firebase.firestore.DocumentSnapshot;
import com.google.firebase.firestore.FirebaseFirestore;

public class CallReceiver extends BroadcastReceiver {

    private static final String TAG = "SafeHer_CallReceiver";
    private FirebaseFirestore db;

    @Override
    public void onReceive(Context context, Intent intent) {
        // Initialize Firestore instance
        db = FirebaseFirestore.getInstance();

        // Check if the broadcast is for a phone state change
        if (intent.getAction().equals(TelephonyManager.ACTION_PHONE_STATE_CHANGED)) {
            String state = intent.getStringExtra(TelephonyManager.EXTRA_STATE);

            if (TelephonyManager.EXTRA_STATE_RINGING.equals(state)) {
                // Fetch the incoming phone number
                String incomingNumber = intent.getStringExtra(TelephonyManager.EXTRA_INCOMING_NUMBER);

                if (incomingNumber != null && !incomingNumber.isEmpty()) {
                    Log.d(TAG, "Incoming Call Detected From: " + incomingNumber);
                    checkIfNumberIsFraud(context, incomingNumber);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private void checkIfNumberIsFraud(Context context, String phoneNumber) {
        // Look up the exact document matching the phone number in your 'fraud_registry' collection
        db.collection("fraud_registry").document(phoneNumber)
                .get()
                .addOnCompleteListener(new OnCompleteListener<DocumentSnapshot>() {
                    @Override
                    public void onComplete(@NonNull Task<DocumentSnapshot> task) {
                        if (task.isSuccessful()) {
                            DocumentSnapshot document = task.getResult();
                            if (document.exists()) {
                                // Number is registered as a fraud number! Extract data.
                                Long reportCount = document.getLong("report_count");
                                String riskLevel = document.getString("risk_level");
                                
                                Log.w(TAG, "FRAUD DETECTED! Risk Level: " + riskLevel);
                                
                                // Action Trigger: For now, we show a Warning Toast.
                                // Later, replace this with your floating XML custom window overlay trigger!
                                Toast.makeText(context, 
                                        "⚠️ WARNING: High Risk Fraud Call Detected (" + riskLevel + ")", 
                                        Toast.LENGTH_LONG).show();
                                
                            } else {
                                Log.d(TAG, "Number is clean or not yet reported.");
                            }
                        } else {
                            Log.e(TAG, "Error checking Firestore database: ", task.getException());
                        }
                    }
                });
    }
}
2. Android Manifest Setup (AndroidManifest.xml)
For security reasons, Android strictly locks down phone-related triggers. You must register your receiver and explicitly request permissions in your AndroidManifest.xml file. Add these lines:

XML
<!-- 1. Add the necessary permissions above the <application> tag -->
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_PHONE_STATE" />

<!-- 2. Inside the <application> tag, register your BroadcastReceiver -->
<receiver android:name=".CallReceiver" android:exported="true">
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.PHONE_STATE_CHANGED" />
    </intent-filter>
</receiver>
3. Critical Step: Requesting Runtime Permissions
Since Android 9, simply putting READ_PHONE_STATE in the Manifest isn't enough. Your app will crash unless you ask the user explicitly when they open the app.

Add this code snippet inside the onCreate method of your main activity (e.g., MainActivity.java):

Java
import android.Manifest;
import android.content.pm.PackageManager;
import androidx.core.app.ActivityCompat;
import androidx.core.content.ContextCompat;

// Inside onCreate:
if (ContextCompat.checkSelfPermission(this, Manifest.permission.READ_PHONE_STATE) 
        != PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED) {
    
    ActivityCompat.requestPermissions(this, 
            new String[]{Manifest.permission.READ_PHONE_STATE}, 101);
}
